{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2953","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2953","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2953\",\"parent_revision\":\"8a964d9434a015b6300f7a037d4b863c880a8c05\",\"root_revision\":\"a7b9f7bc26a220d6804282d4193094e3aae803ca8b5a2a708a8ba70207ddf8b4\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Refactor\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-17\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`github_contents.py` keeps 5 custom Protocol shims so test doubles can structurally satisfy PyGithub types, because PyGithub's real classes have private, requester-bound constructors that a test double cannot nominally satisfy (see PR #2939). PyGithub is fully typed (`py.typed`, confirmed), so the Protocols are not a missing-types workaround \u2014 they exist only because tests cannot construct real PyGithub objects directly.\\n\\nPyGithub's own test suite avoids this by mocking at the HTTP layer instead of the class layer: a `Framework.TestCase` + `ReplayData` system that intercepts requests and replays recorded responses, so tests exercise genuine `Repository`/`ContentFile` objects built from real API responses rather than hand-rolled doubles. PyGithub 2.9.0 (pinned in this repo) depends on `requests` for transport, so an HTTP-layer recorder such as `vcrpy` or `responses` could in principle replace the 5 Protocol shims with real PyGithub objects constructed from a one-time recorded cassette against the real GitHub API.\\n\\n**User-provided context**: This is a proposed testing-architecture improvement to investigate and prototype, not a confirmed defect. Feasibility is unverified \u2014 specifically whether `github_contents.py`'s test setup routes all PyGithub calls through `requests` in a way `vcrpy` can intercept cleanly.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:55:28.419119Z</sub>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:55:28.419119Z</sub>\\n\\n## Claim 1: \\\"`github_contents.py` keeps 5 custom Protocol shims\\\"\\n\\n**VERDICT: REFUTED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py` (lines 80, 88, 93, 98, 109, 117, 123)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18\\n- Content: Actual Protocol count verified via grep:\\n  ```\\n  80:  class _ContentsFile(Protocol):\\n  88:  class _Commit(Protocol):\\n  93:  class _Branch(Protocol):\\n  98:  class _GitTreeEntry(Protocol):\\n  109: class _GitTree(Protocol):\\n  117: class _Requester(Protocol):\\n  123: class _ContentsRepository(Protocol):\\n  ```\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** The file contains 7 custom Protocol shim classes, not 5. The missing count includes `_Requester` and `_ContentsRepository` which are essential to the implementation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Claim 2: \\\"PyGithub's real classes have private, requester-bound constructors that a test double cannot nominally satisfy\\\"\\n\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: PyGithub 2.9.0 installed package (`/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/.venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/github/`)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18\\n- Content: Inspection of constructor signatures for all major classes:\\n  ```\\n  ContentFile.__init__(self, requester: 'Requester', headers: ..., attributes: ..., ...)\\n  Repository.__init__(self, requester: 'Requester', headers: ..., attributes: ..., ...)\\n  Commit.__init__(self, requester: 'Requester', headers: ..., attributes: ..., ...)\\n  Branch.__init__(self, requester: 'Requester', headers: 'dict[str, str | int]', attributes: 'dict[str, Any]')\\n  GitTree.__init__(self, requester: 'Requester', headers: ..., attributes: ..., ...)\\n  GitTreeElement.__init__(self, requester: 'Requester', headers: 'dict[str, str | int]', attributes: 'dict[str, Any]')\\n  ```\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** All PyGithub classes require a `requester` parameter that is internal to PyGithub and cannot be easily constructed by test doubles without the full PyGithub infrastructure.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Claim 3: \\\"PyGithub is fully typed (`py.typed`, confirmed)\\\"\\n\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: PyGithub 2.9.0 installed package marker file\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18\\n- Command: `python3 -c \\\"import github; import os; site_pkg = os.path.dirname(github.__file__); py_typed = os.path.join(site_pkg, 'py.typed'); print(f'py.typed exists: {os.path.exists(py_typed)}')\\\"` \u2192 `py.typed exists: True`\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** The PEP 561 marker file is present, confirming full type annotations throughout PyGithub.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Claim 4: \\\"PyGithub 2.9.0 (pinned in this repo)\\\"\\n\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/pyproject.toml` (lines 9, 34)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18\\n- Content: `pygithub>=2.9.0` declared in both `dependencies` and `[dependency-groups] dev`\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** The repo pins PyGithub to version 2.9.0 or newer.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Claim 5: \\\"PyGithub 2.9.0 depends on `requests` for transport\\\"\\n\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: PyGithub 2.9.0 `Requester.py` import section (lines 17-18 of imports)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18\\n- Content:\\n  ```python\\n  import requests\\n  import requests.adapters\\n  from urllib3 import Retry\\n  ```\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** PyGithub's Requester module directly imports `requests` and `requests.adapters`, confirming `requests` is the transport layer.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Claim 6: \\\"PyGithub's own test suite avoids this by mocking at the HTTP layer instead of the class layer: a `Framework.TestCase` + `ReplayData` system\\\"\\n\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: PyGithub GitHub repository (main branch, `tests/Framework.py`)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18 via WebFetch\\n- Content: Framework.py contains `BasicTestCase` and `TestCase` classes with `RecordingHttpConnection`, `RecordingHttpsConnection`, `ReplayingHttpConnection`, and `ReplayingHttpsConnection` classes that intercept HTTP requests and replay from stored data files. Test methods automatically map to replay data files via `__openFile` logic.\\n\\n**CROSS_CHECK:** Installed PyGithub package does not include the `github.tests` module, confirming this testing infrastructure is internal-only to PyGithub's own test suite and not exposed to consumers of the installed package.\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** PyGithub's test framework mocks at the HTTP transport layer using recorded/replayed HTTP interactions rather than constructing real class instances. This pattern is not exposed as a public API for consumers.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:48:05.121856Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: refactor: backlog_core's github_contents.py test doubles use custom Protocol shims instead of PyGithub's own HTTP-replay testing pattern\\nDate: 2026-08-18\\nGoal: Determine whether and how to replace github_contents.py's Protocol-shim test doubles with HTTP-layer replay testing so tests exercise genuine PyGithub objects.\\nConditions:\\n1. Does github_contents.py's test setup route PyGithub calls through requests in a way vcrpy/responses could intercept? | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. Are vcrpy/responses already dependencies, and would adding one conflict with this repo's dependency conventions (no uv workspace, PEP 723)? | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. Does PyGithub publish its own reusable ReplayData/Framework.TestCase test utilities? | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. How many Protocol shims actually exist in github_contents.py (item states 5; investigation found 7)? | Status: AVAILABLE\\n5. Which of the three viable implementation paths (live-recorded cassettes, responses-based matchers, or hybrid) best fits this repo's test-suite performance and CI-credential constraints? | Status: DERIVABLE\\nAVAILABLE count: 4\\nDERIVABLE count: 1\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:54:03.215742Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: unbounded-design\\n\\n**Rationale**: The item proposes replacing hand-rolled Protocol shims with HTTP-layer replay testing, but no single implementation path is established and feasibility is explicitly unverified. Multiple valid approaches exist (three documented), each with different tradeoffs around maintainability, test speed, and brittleness to API changes. The problem space requires design framing and trade-off evaluation before planning can proceed.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: design-framing\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Test doubles with 5 custom Protocol shims requiring maintenance when PyGithub updates \u2192 Real PyGithub objects constructed from recorded HTTP cassettes, reducing test-double maintenance while exercising actual API contracts\\n</div>\\n\\n## Research\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:52:07.953167Z</sub>\\n\\nResearch consolidated from the discovery-gate investigation (`dh:codebase-analyzer`, 2026-08-18) \u2014 reused here rather than re-running Wave 0's technical-researcher, since the same technology/concern (PyGithub HTTP transport, vcrpy/responses feasibility, github_contents.py's test-double structure) was already covered in depth. Full detail lives in the registered `feature-context` artifact for this item.\\n\\n**API state (PyGithub 2.9.0, pinned)**: Production `Requester.py` imports `requests` directly and routes 100% of HTTP calls through `requests.Session` (with `urllib3.Retry` used only for connection-level retry, not as an alternate transport). No alternate transport path exists. PyGithub does not publish its own `Framework.TestCase`/`ReplayData` test utilities to consumers \u2014 any HTTP-layer replay harness must be custom-built in this repo.\\n\\n**Ecosystem (vcrpy / responses)**: Neither is currently a declared dependency anywhere in this repo. Both are structurally compatible with PyGithub's `requests`-based transport \u2014 `vcrpy` intercepts at the `requests.Session` level (exactly where PyGithub sends its calls) and `responses` patches `requests.adapters` directly. No known incompatibility with PyGithub's retry/session-pooling logic was found. Either could be added as a dev-only dependency (root `pyproject.toml [dependency-groups] dev`) without conflicting with this repo's \\\"no uv workspace\\\" / PEP 723 self-resolving script convention.\\n\\n**Codebase audit (`github_contents.py` + its tests)**: 7 Protocol shims exist at `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py:79-138` (`_ContentsFile`, `_Commit`, `_Branch`, `_GitTreeEntry`, `_GitTree`, `_Requester`, `_ContentsRepository`) \u2014 not the 5 stated in the original item description. `_ContentsFile` is `@runtime_checkable` because `put()` performs an `isinstance()` check against it at runtime. The current test suite (`plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_contents.py:31-206`) mocks entirely at the `Repository`/`Requester` method level via hand-rolled Pydantic/custom classes \u2014 it does not touch HTTP at all today. Adopting HTTP-layer replay therefore requires switching tests to construct real PyGithub objects, not simply layering `vcrpy` on top of the existing fakes.\\n\\n**Impact measurement**: Three viable implementation paths identified, none yet selected (open design question for planning): (A) live-recorded cassettes replayed in CI, (B) cassette-free `responses`-based request matchers, (C) hybrid \u2014 keep in-memory fakes as the default and use `vcrpy` only for cassette recording/refresh during development.\\n\\nSTATUS: DONE (research reused from discovery gate \u2014 see feature-context artifact `plan/feature-context-github-contents-http-replay-tests.md` for full citations and the resolved-questions table)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 2: \\\"Pygithub'S Real Classes Have Private, Requester-Bound Constructors That A Test Double Cannot Nominally Satisfy\\\"\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: PyGithub 2.9.0 installed package (`/Users/jamienelso