ibility, github_contents.py's test-double structure) was already covered in depth. Full detail lives in the registered `feature-context` artifact for this item.\\n\\n**API state (PyGithub 2.9.0, pinned)**: Production `Requester.py` imports `requests` directly and routes 100% of HTTP calls through `requests.Session` (with `urllib3.Retry` used only for connection-level retry, not as an alternate transport). No alternate transport path exists. PyGithub does not publish its own `Framework.TestCase`/`ReplayData` test utilities to consumers \u2014 any HTTP-layer replay harness must be custom-built in this repo.\\n\\n**Ecosystem (vcrpy / responses)**: Neither is currently a declared dependency anywhere in this repo. Both are structurally compatible with PyGithub's `requests`-based transport \u2014 `vcrpy` intercepts at the `requests.Session` level (exactly where PyGithub sends its calls) and `responses` patches `requests.adapters` directly. No known incompatibility with PyGithub's retry/session-pooling logic was found. Either could be added as a dev-only dependency (root `pyproject.toml [dependency-groups] dev`) without conflicting with this repo's \\\"no uv workspace\\\" / PEP 723 self-resolving script convention.\\n\\n**Codebase audit (`github_contents.py` + its tests)**: 7 Protocol shims exist at `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py:79-138` (`_ContentsFile`, `_Commit`, `_Branch`, `_GitTreeEntry`, `_GitTree`, `_Requester`, `_ContentsRepository`) \u2014 not the 5 stated in the original item description. `_ContentsFile` is `@runtime_checkable` because `put()` performs an `isinstance()` check against it at runtime. The current test suite (`plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_contents.py:31-206`) mocks entirely at the `Repository`/`Requester` method level via hand-rolled Pydantic/custom classes \u2014 it does not touch HTTP at all today. Adopting HTTP-layer replay therefore requires switching tests to construct real PyGithub objects, not simply layering `vcrpy` on top of the existing fakes.\\n\\n**Impact measurement**: Three viable implementation paths identified, none yet selected (open design question for planning): (A) live-recorded cassettes replayed in CI, (B) cassette-free `responses`-based request matchers, (C) hybrid \u2014 keep in-memory fakes as the default and use `vcrpy` only for cassette recording/refresh during development.\\n\\nSTATUS: DONE (research reused from discovery gate \u2014 see feature-context artifact `plan/feature-context-github-contents-http-replay-tests.md` for full citations and the resolved-questions table)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 3: \\\"Pygithub Is Fully Typed (`Py.Typed`, Confirmed)\\\"\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: PyGithub 2.9.0 installed package marker file\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18\\n- Command: `python3 -c \\\"import github; import os; site_pkg = os.path.dirname(github.__file__); py_typed = os.path.join(site_pkg, 'py.typed'); print(f'py.typed exists: {os.path.exists(py_typed)}')\\\"` \u2192 `py.typed exists: True`\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** The PEP 561 marker file is present, confirming full type annotations throughout PyGithub.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **maintainer of the codebase**, I want to **backlog_core's github_contents.py test doubles use custom protocol shims instead of pygithub's own http-replay testing pattern** so that **the code is cleaner and more maintainable**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 6: \\\"Pygithub'S Own Test Suite Avoids This By Mocking At The Http Layer Instead Of The Class Layer: A `Framework.Testcase` + `Replaydata` System\\\"\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: PyGithub GitHub repository (main branch, `tests/Framework.py`)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18 via WebFetch\\n- Content: Framework.py contains `BasicTestCase` and `TestCase` classes with `RecordingHttpConnection`, `RecordingHttpsConnection`, `ReplayingHttpConnection`, and `ReplayingHttpsConnection` classes that intercept HTTP requests and replay from stored data files. Test methods automatically map to replay data files via `__openFile` logic.\\n\\n**CROSS_CHECK:** Installed PyGithub package does not include the `github.tests` module, confirming this testing infrastructure is internal-only to PyGithub's own test suite and not exposed to consumers of the installed package.\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** PyGithub's test framework mocks at the HTTP transport layer using recorded/replayed HTTP interactions rather than constructing real class instances. This pattern is not exposed as a public API for consumers.\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 1: \\\"`Github Contents.Py` Keeps 5 Custom Protocol Shims\\\"\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**VERDICT: REFUTED**\\n\\n**EVIDENCE:**\\n- Source: `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py` (lines 80, 88, 93, 98, 109, 117, 123)\\n- Retrieved: 2026-08-18\\n- Content: Actual Protocol count verified via grep:\\n  ```\\n  80:  class _ContentsFile(Protocol):\\n  88:  class _Commit(Protocol):\\n  93:  class _Branch(Protocol):\\n  98:  class _GitTreeEntry(Protocol):\\n  109: class _GitTree(Protocol):\\n  117: class _Requester(Protocol):\\n  123: class _ContentsRepository(Protocol):\\n  ```\\n\\n**EXPLANATION:** The file contains 7 custom Protocol shim classes, not 5. The missing count includes `_Requester` and `_ContentsRepository` which are essential to the implementation.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-17T23:56:17.603378Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_contents.py::_ContentsFile, _Commit, _Branch, _GitTreeEntry, _GitTree, _Requester, _ContentsRepository` (lines 79-138) - the 7 Protocol shims are the direct subject of the refactor. The large inline comment (lines 61-78) is itself PR #2939's doc-only rationale \u2014 it must be rewritten (or removed if shims are eliminated) to describe whichever of the three candidate paths (A/B/C from the feature-context artifact) is implemented, since its current claim (\\\"these five Protocols... exist because the real classes have private, requester-bound constructors\\\") describes exactly the constraint an HTTP-replay approach works around. Note: the comment says \\\"five\\\" but 7 Protocols actually exist \u2014 a pre-existing inline discrepancy independent of this refactor. | Risk: HIGH | Why: central refactor target; any path (A/B/C) requires either removing/reducing Protocols or leaving them for the hybrid path, and the isinstance() check on `_ContentsFile` at line 284 (`@runtime_checkable`) has runtime behavior implications if swapped for a real `isinstance(x, ContentFile)` check.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/gh_client.py::_GraphQLCapable, _GraphQLRequester, _graphql_request` (lines 58-72, 484) - `_fetch_blobs_graphql` in github_contents.py calls `gh_client._graphql_request(repository, query, variables)` against `repository.requester.graphql_query`. The `_GraphQLCapable` docstring (line 65-68) explicitly cites `github_contents.py's _ContentsRepository Protocol, used for test-fake substitution` as its motivating example \u2014 if the substitution mechanism changes from structural-Protocol fakes to HTTP-replayed real objects, this docstring's stated rationale becomes stale even though the Protocol itself is unaffected. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: documentation/comment staleness in a producer that is explicitly cross-referenced by the file under refactor; no behavioral risk since `_graphql_request` itself is unchanged.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py::GitHubBackend.__init__` (line 135) - composition root: `self._contents = contents or _GitHubContentsStore(self.get_github)`. Production code is unaffected (no signature change), but the constructor's `contents=` injection seam is exactly what `tests/test_github_contents.py` (and `test_github_content_safety.py`'s narrower fakes) exploit to substitute fakes \u2014 any change to how `_GitHubContentsStore` is faked/replayed must continue to satisfy this same injection point, or `GitHubBackend`'s own tests (`test_malformed_native_content_does_not_fall_back_to_legacy`, `test_backend_prefers_v1_content_and_falls_back_to_legacy`, etc., which construct `GitHubBackend(..., contents=store)`) break. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: no production code change expected, but multiple existing tests depend on the current `contents=` fake-injection contract remaining compatible with whichever new test-double/replay object replaces `store`.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\nNone identified beyond the producer/consumer entries above \u2014 no type aliases, exported schemas, or generated manifests reference the 7 Protocol shim names outside `github_contents.py` and its test file.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_contents.py` (lines 31-230, all 27 test functions) - defines the full current Protocol-shim double set (`_File`, `_TreeEntry`, `_Tree`, `_FakeRequester`, `_Repository`, `_FakeCommit`, `_FakeBranch`, `_PagedContent`) and every test in the file constructs `_GitHubContentsStore` against the `_Repository` fake via the `store`/`repository` fixtures (lines 223-230). This is the direct migration target: under path A/B, all fakes are replaced by real PyGithub objects (cassette- or matcher-backed); under path C (hybrid), these fakes remain the default and only cassette-recording/refresh tooling is added alongside them. Existing regression-guard tests (`test_content_branch_creation_false_conflict_fails_closed`, `test_fake_repository_rejects_content_ops_against_unbootstrapped_branch`, etc.) encode specific fake behaviors (e.g. `_require_branch`) that must be reproduced faithfully by any replacement double or cassette 